      "narration": "The second stage exists because of a problem that has no direct solution. We want the model to be helpful, honest and harmless. None of those can be written as a loss function — you cannot differentiate helpfulness, and nobody can specify it precisely enough to code. But here is the observation the whole field is built on: while people cannot define quality, people are quite reliable at comparing. Show someone two answers to the same question and ask which is better, and you get a consistent signal, from annotators who could never have written a specification. So you collect comparisons rather than scores. Absolute ratings drift — one person's seven is another's five, and the same person's seven drifts over an afternoon. Pairwise preferences are stable. Classic RLHF then has three steps. You already have the instruction-tuned model from stage one. Step one: sample several responses per prompt and have humans rank them, giving you a dataset of preferred and rejected pairs. Step two: train a reward model — usually the same architecture with a scalar head — to predict which of two responses a human would pick. That reward model is a learned stand-in for a person, and it is what lets you get a score for a response nobody has ever looked at. Step three: optimize the language model to produce responses the reward model scores highly, using PPO, a reinforcement learning algorithm. And there is a term in that optimization doing essential work: a KL divergence penalty that pulls the model back toward the instruction-tuned model it started from. Without it, the policy discovers that the reward model is a model, with quirks and blind spots, and drifts into whatever maximizes it — which in practice is degenerate text that scores beautifully and reads like nothing a person would write. The KL term says: get a higher reward, but do not become a different model to do it. Now, this pipeline works and it produced ChatGPT, and it is also genuinely painful. Three models in memory at once, a reinforcement learning loop that is famously unstable, and a pile of hyperparameters that interact. So the practical story has a second act. Direct Preference Optimization observed that the reward model and the RL step can be collapsed algebraically. If you write down what the optimal policy under a KL-constrained reward objective looks like, you can rearrange it into a simple loss over the preference pairs themselves — one that raises the likelihood of the preferred response and lowers the likelihood of the rejected one, relative to the reference model. No reward model. No sampling loop. No PPO. It is a supervised loss you can train with the same code as stage one, it is stable, and on most benchmarks it performs comparably. That is why nearly every open-weight model released recently is DPO-tuned or one of its variants. The insight to keep is not the algebra: it is that the expensive part was never the algorithm. It is the preference data, and both methods need exactly the same data.",
