      "narration": "Luffy is the clearest illustration of this distinction I know, and once you see it the whole lesson holds together. He arrives on the ship with more raw capability than anyone on it. That is not in question and it never becomes the problem. What he also arrives with is a set of defaults that would get everybody killed: charge in first, take every fight offered, ignore what the sky is doing, announce the plan to the person you are about to fight, eat before checking whether the food is trapped. Enormous power, and no judgement whatsoever about when to deploy it. That is a base model after pretraining. Now watch what the crew actually does over the next several hundred episodes, because the negative is as important as the positive. They never train his strength. There is no arc where Nami makes Luffy punch harder — she could not, and it would not help. What they do is correct choices, one at a time, in the moment. Not now. Not that one. Go around. Wait for Zoro. And notice the form the correction takes, because it is exactly the shape of the data we just described. It is almost never an abstract rule, because nobody can write down the rule for when Luffy should charge in — the crew certainly cannot, and they have tried. It is comparative and situated: this option, not that one, here. That is a preference pair, and it is collected the same way, by someone who knows a better answer when they see it and could not have specified it in advance. Then the part of the analogy I like most, because it maps onto the piece of RLHF that seems most abstract. Nami cannot be standing next to Luffy at every decision. So what he actually ends up carrying is not her corrections — it is an internalized model of what Nami would say. He pauses, briefly, and something in him scores the option the way she would have. That is the reward model exactly: a learned stand-in for a human judge, trained on their past comparisons, available to evaluate situations the human has never seen. And the crew's judgement generalizes to islands Nami has never visited, which is precisely what you need a reward model for. Finally, the constraint, and it is the most important part. Through all of that correction, Luffy stays Luffy. If the crew succeeded in making him cautious, they would have destroyed the thing that makes them survive anything at all — the willingness to attack a problem that a sensible person would walk away from. The corrections are meant to shape when he charges, not to stop him charging. That is the KL penalty, and it is not a technical footnote. Over-optimize a model against a preference signal and you get something that is agreeable, hedging, refuses reasonable requests, and has quietly lost the capability you were paying for. An aligned Luffy who never charges in is not an improvement. He is a different and much less useful crew member.",
